    "---\n",
    "<a id='7'></a>\n",
    "## 7. Main findings and pipeline handoff\n",
    "\n",
    "### Cleaning decisions\n",
    "\n",
    "| Issue | What I found | What the pipeline should do |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| Dirty `Activity Level` labels | 6 raw variants but only 3 real classes | Map to clean labels |\n",
    "| Dirty `HVAC Operation Mode` labels | 23 raw variants but 6 real modes | Lowercase, strip spaces, replace underscores |\n",
    "| Impossible temperature | 795 readings are unrealistically high | Set `Temperature > 40` to missing, then median-impute |\n",
    "| Impossible humidity | 414 readings outside 0-100% | Set invalid values to missing, then median-impute |\n",
    "| Missing values | Four columns affected, from 8.3% to 19.3% | Median for numeric, mode for categorical |\n",
    "| Class imbalance | High Activity is only 10.9% | Stratified split, F1 metrics, class weights/SMOTE comparison |\n",
    "| `Session ID` | Identifier, not a useful sensor feature | Drop before training |\n",
    "\n",
    "### Features I expect to matter most\n",
    "1. `MetalOxideSensor_Unit2` and `MetalOxideSensor_Unit4`\n",
    "2. `CO2_ElectroChemicalSensor`\n",
    "3. `CO_GasSensor`\n",
    "4. `TotalMOS`\n",
    "5. `Time of Day` and `Ambient Light Level` as supporting context features\n",
    "\n",
    "### Features to treat carefully\n",
    "- `Temperature` and `Humidity` should be cleaned and kept at first, but I do not expect them to be top predictors.\n",
    "- `CO2_InfraredSensor` may be partly redundant with `CO2_ElectroChemicalSensor`.\n",
    "- `CO2_CO_Ratio` is useful to test, but should only remain if validation performance improves.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "### Metrics to report\n",
    "- **Weighted F1-score** as the main score because classes are imbalanced.\n",
    "- **Macro F1-score** to check whether minority classes are being ignored.\n",
    "- **High Activity recall** because missing the rare high-activity class would be a problem.\n",
    "- **Confusion matrix** to see where the model is mixing up Low, Moderate and High Activity."
